{
  "evidence": [
    {
      "chunk_id": "sha256:cc034eb4677c24cb7575ecedccda457993c6ad41907229626e849b979cd9a015",
      "quote": "If None or a RandomState instance is passed: fit and split will yield different results each time they are called, and the succession of calls explores all sources of entropy."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "sha256:1a0406bacf590bcfd43c8c944a87b8fbb095df3efea05098295bb5cc62991d10",
      "quote": "The Random Number Generator (RNG) of the estimator is consumed (i.e. mutated) when fit is called, and this mutated RNG will be used in the subsequent calls to fit."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "sha256:73aaf267250e56e26d414ab174b8049389d500406b432120d22948485903982e",
      "quote": "If an integer were passed, a and b would be exact clones and they would not influence each other."
    }
  ],
  "decision": "answer",
  "answer": "When reusing a RandomState instance, the Random Number Generator (RNG) is consumed (mutated) during each fit call, causing different results even with identical data. With an integer seed, the RNG is reset to its original state at the beginning of each fit call, ensuring reproducibility.",
  "abstention_reason": ""
}
